WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.380
Welcome back, everyone. Look, if you have ever been trapped in one of those endless, absolutely agonizing Slack debates about what a feature was actually supposed to do, or if you've ever had a developer hand over a finished product that looks literally nothing like what you envisioned, you are in exactly the right place today. In this explainer, we're on a mission to completely eliminate ambiguity in your product requirements. We're going to take those vague, abstract ideas and transform them into bulletproof, measurable, code-level criteria.

2
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.660
Let's make sure we're building exactly what we intend to, every single time.

3
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.940
Okay, let's just quickly dive into our roadmap for today.

4
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.320
We're going to start by looking at 1. Preventing Startup Failure.

5
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.280
Then we'll move right into 2. Defining What and Why.

6
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.380
3. Setting Quantitative Metrics.

7
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.200
4. The Given When Then Framework.

8
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.360
And finally, we'll wrap up with 5. Socratic Stress Testing.

9
00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:54.670
Alright, Section 1. Preventing the Number 1 Reason for Startup Failure.

10
00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.130
Before we even think about writing a single line of code, or heck, even writing a requirement,

11
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:03.330
we need to confront a pretty terrifying reality about why most companies fail

12
00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.730
before they even get off the ground.

13
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:09.630
According to CB Insights, the absolute number one reason startups crash and burn

14
00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.730
is simply no market need.

15
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.270
Think about that for a second.

16
00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.090
It's building an excellent, flawless product for a problem

17
00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.930
that nobody's actually willing to pay to solve.

18
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.390
It's kind of like a military operation, right?

19
00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:24.190
No commander in their right mind plans an operation with solid intelligence.

20
00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.150
They need to know what's happening in the arena,

21
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.650
what forces are operating, and where the window of opportunity actually is.

22
00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.450
Building a product without validating that market need,

23
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.930
it's basically like deploying troops completely in the dark.

24
00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.290
So that crucial intelligence gathering,

25
00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:40.470
that's what we call the market requirements document, or the MRD.

26
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.210
This document exists purely to prove there is a battle actually worth fighting

27
00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.370
before you move a single developer or designer.

28
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.230
It defines the size of the market using real numbers, not just gut feelings.

29
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.210
It pinpoints exactly what the customer is trying to achieve,

30
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:54.690
rather than just what they're asking for.

31
00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:59.630
Honestly, think of the MRD not as just more corporate bureaucracy, but as incredibly cheap

32
00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:00.110
insurance.

33
00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.090
It's insurance against building a great product for a completely non-existent market.

34
00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:09.210
Let's move on and see how this intelligence actually builds over time through this chronological

35
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:09.570
funnel.

36
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.610
You start at the top with the MRD to figure out the why and who.

37
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.190
Only then do you write the PRD, the product requirements document, to answer the what.

38
00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.770
Then comes the design spec for how it looks, the tech spec for how it's actually built,

39
00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.370
And finally, the user stories for your specific sprints.

40
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:26.810
But here's the catch.

41
00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.470
If you skip that very first MRD step,

42
00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.170
every single document that follows is essentially built on thin air.

43
00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:37.850
Moving to section two, defining the what and why.

44
00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.190
So once we know for sure we have a valid market,

45
00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.930
how do we translate those broad market needs

46
00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.650
into actual tangible product requirements?

47
00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.870
Now, what's really interesting about this

48
00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.970
is the stark contrast between these two approaches.

49
00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:57.170
The MRD on the left, it's focused entirely outward, on the market, the customer, the competition.

50
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.150
Its main audience is your executives and strategy teams.

51
00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.610
But the PRD on the right shifts that focus entirely.

52
00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:08.010
It focuses inward, on the solution, the features, the acceptance criteria.

53
00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.430
Its audience is your developers, your designers, your QA testers.

54
00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.590
The PRD clearly defines the what and the why, and explicitly, and I mean explicitly,

55
00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.770
leaves the how up to your development team.

56
00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:23.130
If you're looking for a super lean, effective way to structure your PRD,

57
00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.930
Lenny's seven questions framework is fantastic.

58
00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.090
It just asks, what's the problem?

59
00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.210
Who's the audience?

60
00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.110
Why now?

61
00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:31.430
What's the high-level solution?

62
00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:32.710
What is out of scope?

63
00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:33.950
How do we measure success?

64
00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:34.870
And what are the risks?

65
00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.390
Notice that out of scope section in particular,

66
00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.450
explicitly stating what you are not going to build

67
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.950
will literally prevent the vast majority of arguments later down the line.

68
00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.630
To really see this in action,

69
00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:50.210
let's look at a real-world case study from our sources featuring McCall. She's a product manager

70
00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:54.670
at a company called Velux, and she was tasked with building a brand new smart notifications feature.

71
00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.950
That brings us to section three, sitting quantitative adoption metrics. Because let's

72
00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.830
be real, if you don't define what success looks like before a single line of code is written,

73
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:08.790
pretty much any result is going to look like a success in hindsight. When Michelle wrote her

74
00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:14.950
PRD for Velux, she didn't just guess that people needed smart notifications. No, she grounded her

75
00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:22.310
document in hard, undeniable data. She found that exactly 38% of VLOX users were actively missing

76
00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:28.090
deadlines specifically because they didn't see an update in time. This number completely proved

77
00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:33.270
the existence of the problem. It wasn't just some PM's gut feeling. It was a highly measurable

78
00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:39.600
pain point. So the crucial point here is how she translated that specific problem into a goal.

79
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.400
On the left, you see a vague kind of fluffy wish, reduce missed deadlines, but on the right,

80
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.680
you see Mikael's actual quantitative adoption metric, a 25% decrease in missed deadlines within

81
00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:58.540
one quarter. A genuinely good PRD is concise and it's decisive. It replaces vague adjectives with

82
00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:05.340
strict, measurable targets that everyone can agree on. Section 4. Eliminating ambiguity with

83
00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:12.010
given when then. Okay, so we have a solid goal, but now we need to bridge that highly dangerous gap

84
00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:18.110
between the PRD and the actual written code. How do we eliminate ambiguity entirely? Well,

85
00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:24.050
we do this through acceptance criteria, or AC. These are the strict, verifiable conditions

86
00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:29.890
that define when a feature is truly, officially complete. Without acceptance criteria, a developer

87
00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:33.950
might mark a ticket as done, but when you actually go in to review it, it's not at all

88
00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:38.750
what you asked for. And I'll let you in on a secret. That gap? It's almost always due to your

89
00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:44.750
vague phrasing, not the developer's negligence. AC is the contract that finally closes the gap

90
00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:49.730
between your intent and their execution. The absolutely most powerful format for writing

91
00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:54.690
these criteria is the Given When Then framework. It's this brilliant rhythmic rule of three.

92
00:05:55.410 --> 00:06:01.070
Given establishes the initial context or the starting state. When describes the specific

93
00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:06.410
action taken by the user and then dictates the exact expected result.

94
00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:11.070
It breaks every single requirement down into a completely unambiguous, highly testable

95
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.950
scenario that QA can easily verify.

96
00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:15.290
Let's look at an example.

97
00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.130
Say Velox needs to add Enterprise Single Sign-On.

98
00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:21.350
A bad requirement simply says SSO should work well.

99
00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:26.950
I mean, to a back-end developer, well might mean it eventually succeeds after 45 agonizing

100
00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:27.950
seconds.

101
00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:29.910
Instead, we use the framework.

102
00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:34.590
Given an Okta user, when an enterprise email is entered, then the login completes in under

103
00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:35.870
five seconds.

104
00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:40.670
By replacing those vague adjectives with precise, hard metrics, you completely eliminate that

105
00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.150
endless ping pong game back and forth with QA.

106
00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.690
And you know, this framework is even more critical for your edge cases.

107
00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:51.990
Writing something like, handle login errors, is basically an open invitation for a developer

108
00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:55.210
to just throw up a blank white screen when things break.

109
00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.290
But Michael applied the format perfectly.

110
00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:01.310
Give in a failed login, then show a clear error message with a support link, ensuring

111
00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.570
no white screen is displayed.

112
00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:07.910
Defining these edge cases up front literally saves weeks of costly, frustrating revisions

113
00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:08.910
down the line.

114
00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.110
Finally, Section 5.

115
00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:15.210
Stress Testing Your Product Requirements There's a realization you really need to

116
00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:16.210
have here.

117
00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:21.010
The exact moment your PRD looks polished, pretty, and ready to share is actually the

118
00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:24.530
most dangerous moment in its entire life cycle.

119
00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:28.570
A document that hasn't been deeply investigated is going to fall apart at its very first review.

120
00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:33.710
You need to use Socratic questioning to stress test your own work, much like they do over at Amazon or Figma.

121
00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:37.350
Ask yourself, is the problem actually real, and how do I know?

122
00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:44.190
Going back to McCall at Velox, she interrogated her own PRD and realized her initial data was based on just 12 support tickets.

123
00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:50.770
Realizing that wasn't nearly enough evidence, she paused, and she validated the problem with a 200-user survey before moving forward.

124
00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:54.870
Which leads directly to the next question you absolutely must ask.

125
00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.050
What is the simplest version of the solution?

126
00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:02.950
And this brilliantly illustrates how Mitchell originally envisioned this massive, complex, real-time notification engine.

127
00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:11.250
But through questioning, she completely swapped that complex engine for a simple daily email digest that could test her hypothesis in just one week of development.

128
00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.470
She saved weeks of engineering time just by starting small.

129
00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:18.970
Here is a challenge you should seriously apply to every single project.

130
00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:23.010
What happens if we only have half the resources to build this?

131
00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:28.110
Answering this forces you to ruthlessly find the absolute core value of your product.

132
00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:32.770
For Meeko, it meant stripping away everything except the single most critical alert,

133
00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:34.090
the deadline change.

134
00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:39.170
Sure, it cuts the scope of the PRD in half, but it makes the remaining feature twice as strong.

135
00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:43.090
So I'm going to leave you with this final, provocative thought to take away from this

136
00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:43.810
explainer today.

137
00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:48.850
If you shared your current product requirements document right now, today, with your harshest

138
00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:53.650
critics, would it survive 15 minutes of relentless Socratic questioning? Would your metrics hold up?

139
00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:58.490
Are your acceptance criteria truly unambiguous? Take the time to scrutinize your own documents,

140
00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:02.430
because by putting in that effort up front, you eliminate the ambiguity, you stop building

141
00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:05.990
features that nobody actually wants, and you ensure that when your team writes code,

142
00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:08.070
they are building exactly what the market demands.

143
00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.650
Thanks for joining me, and I'll catch you on the next explainer.

